::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q01::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the horizontal distance from (3, 4) to (11, 4)?
{
=A. 8 units#Correct. The y-values match, so subtract x-values: 11 - 3 = 8.
~B. 14 units#This adds x-values. Distance is a difference, not a sum.
~C. 7 units#This subtracts 4 from 11. Use the two x-values.
~D. 11 units#This uses one coordinate as the distance. Compare both points.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q02::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the vertical distance from (6, 2) to (6, 9)?
{
~A. 6 units#This uses the shared x-value as distance. Vertical distance uses y-values.
~B. 7 units#Correct. The x-values match, so subtract y-values: 9 - 2 = 7.
=C. 7 units#Correct. The distance is the difference between 9 and 2.
~D. 11 units#This adds the y-values. Distance is not found by adding coordinates.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q03::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (0, 0), (8, 0), (8, 3), and (0, 3). What is its area?
{
~A. 11 square units#This adds width and height. Area requires multiplication.
=B. 24 square units#Correct. Width = 8 and height = 3, so area = 8 x 3 = 24.
~C. 22 square units#This is perimeter, not area.
~D. 24 units#The number is correct, but area requires square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q04::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has side lengths 9 units and 5 units. What is its perimeter?
{
~A. 14 units#This adds each different side only once. Perimeter includes all sides.
~B. 45 square units#This finds area, not perimeter.
~C. 28 square units#The number is correct for perimeter, but the unit should not be square units.
=D. 28 units#Correct. Perimeter = 9 + 5 + 9 + 5 = 28 units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q05::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which point has x-coordinate 7 and y-coordinate 2?
{
~A. (2, 7)#This reverses x and y. Ordered pairs are written (x, y).
=B. (7, 2)#Correct. The first coordinate is x and the second coordinate is y.
~C. (7, 7)#This repeats the x-coordinate as both coordinates.
~D. (2, 2)#This repeats the y-coordinate as both coordinates.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q06::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (1, 8) to (10, 8)?
{
=A. 9 units#Correct. The y-values match, so horizontal distance is 10 - 1 = 9.
~B. 11 units#This adds x-values instead of subtracting them.
~C. 7 units#This compares 1 and 8, not the two x-values.
~D. 10 units#This uses one endpoint's x-value rather than the difference.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q07::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (2, 1), (7, 1), (7, 6), and (2, 6). What is its perimeter?
{
~A. 10 units#This adds width and height only once. Perimeter uses all four sides.
~B. 20 square units#The number is correct for perimeter, but perimeter uses linear units.
~C. 25 square units#This is the area, not the perimeter.
=D. 20 units#Correct. Width = 5 and height = 5, so perimeter = 20 units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q08::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (2, 1), (7, 1), (7, 6), and (2, 6). What is its area?
{
~A. 10 square units#This adds width and height. Area requires multiplication.
~B. 20 square units#This is the perimeter number, not area.
=C. 25 square units#Correct. Width = 5 and height = 5, so area = 25 square units.
~D. 25 units#The number is correct, but area uses square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q09::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which coordinate stays the same for a horizontal segment?
{
=A. y-coordinate#Correct. A horizontal segment has the same y-value at both endpoints.
~B. x-coordinate#The x-coordinate changes for horizontal movement.
~C. both coordinates#If both coordinates stay the same, there is no segment length.
~D. neither coordinate#A horizontal segment keeps the y-coordinate constant.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q10::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which coordinate stays the same for a vertical segment?
{
~A. y-coordinate#The y-coordinate changes during vertical movement.
=B. x-coordinate#Correct. A vertical segment has the same x-value at both endpoints.
~C. both coordinates#If both coordinates stay the same, the points are identical.
~D. neither coordinate#A vertical segment keeps the x-coordinate constant.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q11::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (5, 3) to (5, 12)?
{
~A. 5 units#This uses the shared x-value. Use y-values for vertical distance.
~B. 15 units#This adds the y-values. Distance is the difference.
=C. 9 units#Correct. The distance is 12 - 3 = 9 units.
~D. 12 units#This uses one y-value as the distance, not the difference.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q12::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has width 4 units and height 6 units. What is its area?
{
=A. 24 square units#Correct. Area = 4 x 6 = 24 square units.
~B. 10 square units#This adds the side lengths. Area requires multiplication.
~C. 20 units#This is the perimeter, not area.
~D. 24 units#The number is correct, but area needs square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q13::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (4, 3), (10, 3), (10, 8), and (4, 8). What is the width?
{
~A. 5 units#This is the vertical height, not the horizontal width.
~B. 6 units#Correct. Width = 10 - 4 = 6 units.
~C. 7 units#This subtracts 3 from 10, mixing x and y values.
=D. 6 units#Correct. The horizontal distance is found from the x-values 4 and 10.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q14::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (4, 3), (10, 3), (10, 8), and (4, 8). What is the height?
{
~A. 6 units#This is the horizontal width, not the vertical height.
=B. 5 units#Correct. Height = 8 - 3 = 5 units.
~C. 7 units#This mixes x and y coordinates.
~D. 10 units#This uses one coordinate as a length rather than subtracting.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q15::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the perimeter of a rectangle with width 6 units and height 5 units?
{
~A. 11 units#This adds width and height only once.
~B. 30 square units#This finds area, not perimeter.
=C. 22 units#Correct. Perimeter = 6 + 5 + 6 + 5 = 22 units.
~D. 22 square units#The number is correct, but perimeter uses units, not square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q16::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which answer could represent the area of a polygon on a coordinate grid?
{
~A. 18 units#Units without square labeling usually represent length or perimeter.
~B. 18 cubic units#Cubic units represent volume, not area.
~C. 18 gallons#Gallons represent capacity, not coordinate area.
=D. 18 square units#Correct. Area on a coordinate grid is measured in square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q17::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
Which answer could represent the perimeter of a polygon on a coordinate grid?
{
=A. 30 units#Correct. Perimeter is a distance around a figure, so it uses linear units.
~B. 30 square units#Square units represent area.
~C. 30 cubic units#Cubic units represent volume.
~D. 30 gallons#Gallons represent capacity, not perimeter.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q18::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (0, 6) to (8, 6)?
{
~A. 6 units#This uses the shared y-value. Use the difference in x-values.
=B. 8 units#Correct. The distance is 8 - 0 = 8 units.
~C. 14 units#This adds x and y values instead of comparing x-values.
~D. 0 units#The points are not the same point, so the distance is not zero.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q19::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (0, 0), (12, 0), (12, 4), and (0, 4). What is its perimeter?
{
~A. 16 units#This adds width and height only once.
~B. 48 square units#This is area, not perimeter.
~C. 32 square units#The number is correct for perimeter, but the unit should not be square.
=D. 32 units#Correct. Perimeter = 12 + 4 + 12 + 4 = 32 units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q20::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has vertices (0, 0), (12, 0), (12, 4), and (0, 4). What is its area?
{
~A. 16 square units#This adds width and height. Area requires multiplication.
~B. 32 square units#This is the perimeter number, not area.
=C. 48 square units#Correct. Area = 12 x 4 = 48 square units.
~D. 48 units#The number is correct, but area needs square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q21::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q21
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the first number in an ordered pair called?
{
=A. x-coordinate#Correct. Ordered pairs are written (x, y), so the first number is the x-coordinate.
~B. y-coordinate#The y-coordinate is the second number in an ordered pair.
~C. perimeter#Perimeter is distance around a figure, not part of an ordered pair.
~D. area#Area measures flat space, not coordinate position.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q22::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q22
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the second number in an ordered pair called?
{
~A. x-coordinate#The x-coordinate is the first number in an ordered pair.
=B. y-coordinate#Correct. Ordered pairs are written (x, y), so the second number is the y-coordinate.
~C. width#Width may be found from coordinates, but it is not the second number's name.
~D. height#Height may be found from coordinates, but it is not the second number's name.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q23::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q23
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has width 7 units and height 2 units. What is its area?
{
~A. 9 square units#This adds width and height. Area requires multiplication.
~B. 18 units#This is perimeter, not area.
=C. 14 square units#Correct. Area = 7 x 2 = 14 square units.
~D. 14 units#The number is correct, but area uses square units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q24::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q24
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
A rectangle has width 7 units and height 2 units. What is its perimeter?
{
~A. 9 units#This adds width and height only once.
~B. 14 square units#This is area, not perimeter.
~C. 18 square units#The number is correct for perimeter, but the unit should not be square.
=D. 18 units#Correct. Perimeter = 7 + 2 + 7 + 2 = 18 units.
}
::MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q25::
Question ID: MCR_U06_L05_Quiz_Q25
MLA Standard: MLA.MCR.GM.2
Question:
What is the distance from (9, 2) to (9, 11)?
{
=A. 9 units#Correct. The x-values match, so vertical distance is 11 - 2 = 9.
~B. 11 units#This uses one coordinate as the distance.
~C. 20 units#This adds 9 and 11, which does not find distance.
~D. 7 units#This subtracts 2 from 9, mixing x and y values.
}
